hadiths[22]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2069","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Every prophet has a supplication that is certainly accepted (whatever he asks, Allah Almighty surely grants it), and I wish to reserve my (such) accepted supplication for intercession for my Ummah in the Hereafter."
"2070","Narrated from Sayyiduna Shaddad bin Aws (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The most superior way of seeking forgiveness is (translation): 'O Allah! You are my Lord, there is no true deity except You. You created me and I am Your servant, and I am upon Your covenant and promise as much as I am able. I seek refuge in You from the evil of what I have done. I acknowledge Your favor upon me and I acknowledge my sin, so forgive me, for surely none forgives sins except You.'"" And he said: ""Whoever recites this supplication for forgiveness during the day with firm belief in it, and dies before evening, he will enter Paradise. And whoever recites it at night with complete certainty and dies before morning, he will also enter Paradise."
"2071","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""By Allah! I seek forgiveness from Allah and repent to Him more than seventy times a day (that is, at all times)."
"2072","`Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated two hadiths: one he narrated from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the other is his own saying. He said, ""A believer considers his sins as if he is sitting under a mountain, fearing that it might fall upon him, while a wicked person considers his sin as if a fly passed over his nose."" The narrator Abu Shahab indicated with his hand and then said, ""Allah is even more pleased with the repentance of His servant than a person who reaches a place where he fears for his life (where food and the like are not available), and he falls asleep. When he wakes up, he finds that the mount on which his food and drink were kept is lost. Then hunger and thirst overpower him, or as Allah wills (the narrator is in doubt), and he prays (to Allah) that he may reach his home. Then (at the same place where he was lying) he goes to sleep, and when he wakes up suddenly, he sees that his mount (with all the provisions) is with him."
"2073","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Hudhaifah bin Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go to his bed, he would recite this supplication: ""(O Allah! In Your name I die and I live.)"" And when he would wake up, he would recite this supplication: ""All praise is for Allah who gave us life after causing us to die (i.e., sleep), and to Him is the return (after rising from the graves)."
"2074","Narrated from Sayyiduna Bara’ bin ‘Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go to bed to sleep, he would lie on his right side and recite this supplication: “O Allah, I have submitted my soul to You, I have turned my face fully towards You, I have entrusted all my affairs to You, my reliance is upon You, I desire only Your grace and mercy, and there is no refuge or escape from Your punishment except with You. I believe in Your Book which You have revealed and in Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whom You have sent.”"
"2075","Narrated Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them): Once I stayed overnight at the house of Umm al-Mu'minin Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her), and he mentioned the whole hadith which has already been mentioned (see the Book: Description of Wudu... Chapter: Reciting the Qur'an after breaking Wudu without performing Wudu), and in this hadith he further says that the supplication which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite included these words: ""O Allah! Place light in my heart, light in my eyes, light in my ears, light on my right, light on my left, light above me, light beneath me, and make light for me all around (that is, grant me light in every direction)."
"2076","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When any of you comes to his bed to sleep, he should dust it off, for he does not know what may have entered it after he left. Then he should recite this supplication: 'My Lord! In Your blessed name I lay my side on the bed, and in Your blessed name I will lift it (in the future). If You withhold my soul in this state (if I die), then have mercy on it, and if You release it, then protect it (from sins) as You protect Your righteous servants.'"
"2077","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When any of you prays, he should ask Allah with certainty (that grant me this thing), and he should not say: If You will, then forgive, and if You will, then have mercy on me. For there is no one who can force or compel Allah."
"2078","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The supplication of every person is accepted as long as he does not hasten and say: 'I made supplication (but) it was not accepted.'"
"2079","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to supplicate during times of distress: ""There is no deity except Allah, the Most Forbearing, there is no deity except Allah, the Lord of the Magnificent Throne, there is no deity except Allah, the Lord of the heavens and the Lord of the earth and the Lord of the Noble Throne."
"2080","`Narrated by Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him)` The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) used to seek refuge from severe hardship, reaching of misfortune, the rejoicing of enemies, and the evil of fate. (The narrator of the hadith) Sufyan said that there were three things in the hadith, I have added one more, and now I do not know which one it is among them."
"2081","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him): I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: ""O Allah! For any believer whom I have spoken harshly to, make that harshness a means of closeness to You for him on the Day of Resurrection."
"2082","The conqueror of Persia, Sayyiduna Sa’d bin Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him), says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to command seeking refuge from these five things: “O Allah! I seek refuge in You from miserliness. O Allah! I seek refuge in You from cowardice. O Allah! I seek refuge in You from living till the worst part of old age. O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the trials of the world, that is, the trial of Dajjal. O Allah! I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave.”"
"2083","Narrated from Ummul Momineen Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to say (translation): ""O Allah! I seek refuge in You from laziness and extreme old age, and I seek refuge in You from sin and debt and liability. I seek refuge in You from the trial of the grave and the punishment of the grave. I seek refuge in You from the trial of Hell and the punishment of Hell. I seek refuge in You from the trial of wealth and the trial of poverty, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of the False Messiah (Dajjal). O Allah! I seek refuge in You. O Allah! Wash away my sins with the water of snow and hail, and cleanse my heart from sins as a white garment is cleansed from dirt, and create distance between me and my sins as You have made distance between the east and the west."
"2084","Narrated from Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would often make this supplication: ""O Allah! O our Lord! Grant us goodness in this world and goodness in the Hereafter, and save us from the punishment of Hell."" (Ameen, O Lord of the worlds!)"
"2085","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate: ""Lord of the worlds! Forgive my mistakes, my ignorance, and my excesses in all my affairs, and what You know better than I do. O Allah! Forgive my inadvertent errors, what I did intentionally, my ignorance, and my frivolous acts, for all these are present in me. O Allah! Forgive my past and future sins, my hidden and open sins."
"2086","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Ayyub Ansari and Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever recites the above-mentioned phrase (the declaration of faith: La ilaha illallah...) ten times, will receive a reward as if he has freed one slave from the descendants of Isma'il (peace be upon him)."
"2087","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever recites in a day one hundred times: (La ilaha illallah wahdahu la sharika lahu, lahu’l-mulku wa lahu’l-hamdu wa huwa ‘ala kulli shay’in qadeer) — There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, He is alone, He has no partner, His is the dominion and His is all praise, and He is powerful over everything — he will have the reward of freeing ten slaves, and one hundred good deeds will be written in his record, and one hundred sins will be erased, and he will be safe from Satan that day until evening, and no one will be better than him except the one who recites it more than this."
"2088","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever recites 'Subhan Allah wa bihamdihi' one hundred times in a day, all his sins will be erased, even if (his sins) are as much as the foam of the sea."
"2089","`Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) said that` the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, ""The example of the one who remembers Allah and the one who does not remember Him is like that of the living and the dead. (That is, the one who remembers is alive and the one who does not remember is like the dead.)"
"2090","Narrated Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There are some angels of Allah who roam the roads seeking those who remember Allah. When they find people remembering Allah, they call out to their fellow angels, 'Come here, your objective has been achieved (i.e., those who remember Allah have been found).'"" He further said: ""These angels cover those people with their wings (and) reach up to the lowest heaven (layer upon layer). Allah, although He knows best, asks them, 'What are My servants saying?' The angels reply, '(O Allah!) They are glorifying, magnifying, praising, and exalting You.' Allah says, '(O angels!) Have they seen Me?' The angels say, 'No, by Allah! They have not seen You.' Allah says, 'If they had seen Me, what would have happened?' The angels say, 'If they had seen You, they would have praised, glorified, and exalted You even more intensely.'"" (The Prophet, peace be upon him, said:) ""Then Allah says, '(O angels!) What do they ask of Me?' The angels say, 'They ask You for Paradise.' Allah says, 'Have they seen Paradise?' (those who seek it) The angels say, 'No, they have not seen it.' Allah says, 'If they had seen it, what would have happened?' The angels say, 'If they had seen it, they would have desired it even more intensely.' Then Allah asks the angels, 'From what do they seek refuge?' The angels say, 'They seek refuge from Hell.' Allah says, 'Have they seen Hell?' The angels say, 'No.' Allah says, 'If they had seen it, what would have happened?' The angels say, 'If they had seen it, they would have fled from it and feared it even more intensely.' Then Allah says, '(O angels!) I make you witnesses that I have forgiven these people.' Then one of the angels says, 'Among these people who remember (Allah), there is a man who is not one of them, but came there for some need.' Allah says, 'They are such people that even their companion is not deprived (of forgiveness).'"
